<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olympias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 375–<a href="/years/316-bce">316 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> was a Molossian princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a> I, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. After Alexander's death she fought for her grandson Alexander IV in the wars of the Successors and had Philip III and his wife killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> captured and executed her in 316 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olympias.jpg" alt="Olympias" width="320" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotogeniss · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_olympias_mus_theski.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> was born around 375 BCE, probably at Passaron in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, the eldest daughter of Neoptolemus I, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a>. Her brother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander I of Epirus</a>. Her family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeacidae">Aeacidae</a>, claimed descent from Neoptolemus, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, and were perhaps the only people of Epirus universally recognised as Greek. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> she was first named Polyxena and took the name Myrtale before her marriage as part of an initiation into a mystery cult.</p><p>In 358 BCE Arybbas, who had succeeded Neoptolemus I as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a>, made a treaty with the new king of Macedonia, Philip II, and the alliance was sealed by the marriage of his niece <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> to Philip in 357 BCE. In 356 BCE Philip's racehorse won at the Olympic Games, and Olympias, until then called Myrtale, took the name Olympias. That summer she gave birth to Alexander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the sources, the marriage was stormy, because of Philip's volatility and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>' ambition and jealousy. In 337 BCE Philip married a Macedonian noblewoman, Cleopatra, niece of Attalus, to whom he gave the name Eurydice, and Olympias went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> with Alexander. When Philip was murdered in 336 BCE by Pausanias, one of his bodyguards, Olympias was suspected of having countenanced the killing.</p><p>During Alexander's campaigns she corresponded with him and wielded great influence in Macedonia, causing trouble for the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>. After Alexander died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 323 BCE, his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a> bore a son, Alexander IV. In 317 BCE Olympias allied with the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyperchon">Polyperchon</a> against Antipater's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, who had made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-arrhidaeus">Philip III Arrhidaeus</a> king. Her army won over the army of Adea Eurydice, the wife of Philip III, and in October 317 BCE she captured and executed the pair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> in Pydna. Her life had been promised in the terms of capitulation, but he decided to have her killed, and when the fortress fell the soldiers sent to kill her refused to harm the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Accounts of her death differ: Pausanias says she was stoned to death by the families of her victims, while Justinus says she went out to meet her enemies in royal attire with two maids and made them stab her publicly. Cassander is also said to have denied her body burial rites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a> and Alexander IV were executed a few years later, in 309 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olympias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Macedonians traditionally practised monogamy, but Philip II married seven wives to bind the Upper Macedonian aristocracy and his allies, among them Phila of Elimeia, the Illyrian Audata, Philinna of Larissa and <b>Olympias</b> of Epirus, who bore Alexander.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the regent Antipater died in 319 BCE, his son Cassander fought Polyperchon and <b>Olympias</b> for Macedon and won control by 316 BCE, executing Olympias.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander I, brother of <b>Olympias</b> and uncle of Alexander the Great, campaigned in southern Italy until his death in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Passaron, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Pydna, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.71,20.77&amp;year=-337" aria-label="Passaron on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5062 742 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Olympias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5512 1012h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5512 1012h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.71,20.77&amp;year=-337">What was here in 337 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
